# How do two tactics combine?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "How do you spot a combination that uses two motifs at once? When does a sacrifice actually work?"
next: {"title":"Week 2 review: tactics without labels","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/14-week-2-review-tactics-without-labels.cast"}

## Warm-up: pin, then in-between
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r3k2r/ppp2ppp/3p4/4n3/8/3B4/PPP2PPP/R3R1K1 w kq - 0 1, moves: [f4], arrows: [{from: e1, to: e8}]}
      - {fen: 5rk1/ppp1rppp/8/3N4/8/8/PPP2PPP/3q1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxe7+, Kh8, Rxd1]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: how two tactics combine into one. First, two warm-ups from last week: a pin, and an in-between move.
    draw position

A: Black's knight on e5 is pinned: behind it, on the same file, stands the king.
    draw arrow_0

B: Then I take it. Rook takes e5, check!
    highlight piece_e1

A: No: the pawn on d6 takes back, a rook for a knight. This pinned knight can't run, so attack it with something cheap.
    highlight piece_d6

    erase arrow_0

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win the knight." widget chess line true hints ["The knight is pinned: it can't move.","Which pawn can attack it?"] themes ["pin"] voice a
        right: Pawn to f4. The knight can't step away without exposing the king, and next move the pawn takes it.
        wrong: Don't take yet. Attack the pinned knight with your cheapest piece.

B: A pawn against a pinned knight. That's the cheapest attacker there is.
    highlight piece_f4

A: Second one. Black's queen has just taken yours on d1.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Easy. Rook takes back.
    highlight piece_f1

A: It's your move: that queen isn't going anywhere yet. So first: checks, captures, threats.
    highlight piece_d1

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A capture with check comes first.","Your knight on d5."] themes ["in-between move"] voice a
        right: Knight takes the rook on e7, check. The king goes to h8, and then the rook takes back the queen: a whole rook won.
        wrong: The queen can wait one move. What can you take with check?

B: The check gave Black no time to save the queen.
    highlight piece_e7

A: A pin, and an in-between move. Today, two tactics in one line.
    highlight piece_e7 piece_d1

## Two tactics, one combination
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1q2rk1/ppp2ppp/2n5/1B1N4/8/8/PPPQ1PPP/5RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Ne7+, Nxe7], arrows: [{from: d5, to: e7}, {from: c6, to: e7}]}
      - {fen: r1q2rk1/ppp2ppp/2n5/1B1N4/8/8/PPPQ1PPP/5RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxc6, bxc6, Ne7+, Kh8, Nxc8]}
      - {fen: 2r3k1/pp3ppp/1q6/8/4Q3/8/PP3PPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qe8+, Rxe8, Rxe8#], arrows: [{from: e4, to: e8}]}
      - {fen: 2r3k1/pp3pp1/1q5p/8/4Q3/8/PP3PPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qe8+, Rxe8, Rxe8+, Kh7], highlights: [h6]}
      - {fen: r1q2rk1/ppp2ppp/2B5/3N4/8/8/PPPQ1PPP/5RK1 b - - 0 1}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: teichmann
      title: Chess is 99% tactics
      authors: Edward Winter (Chess Notes)
      year: 2026
      finding: The remark is attributed to Richard Teichmann through Erwin Voellmy's Schachtaktik (1930), which adds that Teichmann exaggerated slightly; it is not in Teichmann's own writings.
      url: https://www.chesshistory.com/winter/extra/teichmann.html

    card title "Two tactics, one combination"

A: So one tactic often needs another. Here's a fork that almost works.
    draw position

B: Knight to e7, check! King and queen at once.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: And the knight on c6 just takes it. The fork square was guarded.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: So ask what stops the fork.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: The guard on c6 is the one thing in the way.
    highlight piece_c6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win the queen." widget chess line true hints ["Remove the guard of e7 first.","Your bishop on b5."] themes ["removing the defender","fork"] voice a
        right: Bishop takes c6. If the pawn takes back, knight to e7, check: the king steps to h8, and the knight takes the queen on c8.
        wrong: The fork on e7 is guarded. Take the guard first.

B: And if Black doesn't take back on c6?
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Then White is simply a piece up. The bishop capture wins either way.
    highlight piece_c6

A: That's a combination: two tactics, where the first move makes the second one work.
    highlight piece_c6 piece_d5

A: To spot one, find the tactic that almost works,
    animate position_shown 0 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: then ask what stops it: a guard, a blocked line, a king on the wrong square.
    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1

A: No wonder Richard Teichmann, a master a century ago, is said to have called chess ninety-nine percent tactics.
    portrait teich cites ["teichmann"] x 90 y 500 width 170 of "Richard Teichmann"
    text cue99 "99%" x 92 y 310 font_size 48
    text cue_tac "tactics" x 92 y 255 font_size 30
    parallel true

A: The friend who reported it added that he exaggerated a little.
    highlight cue99 cue_tac effect underline

    erase teich cue99 cue_tac

A: Second question: when does a sacrifice actually work?
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Queen to e8 is check. But the rook just takes her: nine points for nothing, if that were the end.
    draw arrow_0

    erase arrow_0

A: Play it to the end. Queen to e8, check.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

B: The rook has to take: the king has no square.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Then White's rook takes back on e8. Mate.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: Two tactics again: the queen drags the last guard off the back rank, and the rook mates.
    highlight piece_e8 piece_g8

A: Now change one thing: Black's pawn stands on h6.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Does the queen sacrifice still work?
    draw hl_h6

    quiz "Does the queen sacrifice still work?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 350 buttons_at.y 336 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: No: after the rook takes back on e8 with check, the king steps out to h7, and White has given a queen for a rook.
        wrong: After the rook checks on e8, look at every square next to Black's king.
        * Still mate
        + King escapes
        * Wins a rook

    erase hl_h6

A: Two moves later, the king has slipped out to h7, and White is four points down.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 4 easing linear

B: One little pawn move, and the whole idea is gone.
    highlight piece_h6

A: So a sacrifice works when the forced line ends with more than you gave. Play it to the end, then count.
    highlight piece_h7

## Your turn: three combinations and a skewer
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Four for four. You see the second tactic behind the first.
    good: Most of them. The rest are on your list for next week.
    poor: Combinations take a while to see. Look for the guard first.
    none: The combinations will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r4rk1/ppp2ppp/1q3n2/8/4N3/3B3Q/PPP2PPP/5RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxf6+, gxf6, Qxh7#]}
      - {fen: 3r2k1/pp1q1ppp/8/8/8/8/PP1Q1PPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qxd7]}
      - {fen: 8/pp4p1/6kp/8/8/5B1P/PP3PPK/1r6 w - - 0 1, moves: [Be4+, Kf6, Bxb1]}
      - {fen: r4rk1/ppp1qppp/1b6/8/3R2b1/5N2/PPQ2PPP/2B2RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Bxf3], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1q2rk1/ppp2ppp/2n5/3N4/8/1Q6/PP3PPP/2R2RK1 w - - 0 1}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    text score_big "Your score" x 500 y 400 font_size 100 hidden true
    text hw "One slow game: 15 + 10" x 500 y 450 font_size 56 hidden true
    text hw2 "Its first mistake?" x 500 y 340 font_size 56 hidden true

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now that you've seen two tactics work as one, it's your turn: three combinations and a skewer, scored.
    draw position

    pause 0.3

    ask "Checkmate." widget chess line true hints ["The knight on f6 guards h7.","Take it, with check."] themes ["removing the defender","mating pattern"] voice a test d13
        right: Knight takes f6, check. The pawn takes back, but whatever Black does, the queen mates on h7, with the bishop behind her.
        wrong: Your queen and bishop both aim at h7. What guards it?

B: Take the guard, then mate.
    highlight piece_h7

A: Next: one black piece has two jobs.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["The rook on d8 guards the queen and the back rank.","Your queen."] themes ["overloading","back rank"] voice a test d13
        right: Queen takes d7. If the rook takes back, rook to e8 is mate; if not, you're a queen up.
        wrong: Which black piece is guarding two things at once?

A: An older one now, from the pins and skewers day.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win the rook." widget chess line true hints ["A check on the long diagonal.","Your bishop."] themes ["skewer"] voice a test d13
        right: Bishop to e4, check. The king steps off the diagonal, and the bishop takes the rook on b1.
        wrong: Check the king so that the rook stands behind it on the same line.

A: Last one: you're Black, so the board turns round.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["The knight on f3 is the rook's only guard.","Your bishop on g4."] themes ["removing the defender"] voice a test d13
        right: Bishop takes f3, the rook's only guard. If the pawn takes back, your other bishop takes the rook on d4 for free; if the rook runs, you stay a knight up.
        wrong: Taking the rook now gives three for five. What guards it?

    hide position

    draw score_big

A: You scored {d13.score} of {d13.total}.
    results test d13 retry true

    erase score_big

    show position

A: Now against the computer, and it will defend. One fork here is guarded.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Win three points or more, within four moves.
    explore activity vs_computer goal.kind win_material goal.within 4 goal.amount 3 store g

    hide position

A: So when a tactic almost works, don't drop it:
    text syn_almost "Almost works?" x 500 y 520 font_size 60

A: find what stops it, and remove that first.
    text syn_guard "What stops it?" x 500 y 410 font_size 60

A: And a sacrifice is only as good as the line behind it.
    text syn_end "Play it to the end" x 500 y 300 font_size 60

    erase syn_almost syn_guard syn_end

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    draw hw hw2

